nodes.triage:
In narrow form: policy and customer fields plus a small approved fact base, not a full company brain.

Field experiments show specific context beats generic:
- goal-specific reminders +16% saving, while generic reminders did nothing;
- a loan officer's name helped only repeat clients;
- a human two-way call raised on-time repayment by 34.4 pp LATE [l4cv].

No study tests AI voice with CRM context [l4c].

H8's kill condition (buyers want a point tool that plugs into what they have) is met on desk evidence:
- no buyer was found switching CRM for voice AI;
- point-first adoption: Zoho One is ~5% of Zoho's 1M+ paying customers;
- incumbents bundle voice [l4c, l4cv].

It is an expansion path. Revisit if 5 or more of the first 15 qualified prospects would buy voice only inside a replacement CRM [l4c].

nodes.title:
Proposed: the v1 parity floor taken from Vapi's API is eight items:
- immutable assistant versions with hash and parent, pinned by label;
- a traffic split between versions;
- typed per-component cost on every call plus a query endpoint;
- a webhook envelope with version and call-changing request/response events (assistant-request, tool-calls, transfer, predial) with retries and signing;
- programmable QA (structured outputs, scorecards, simulated personas, latency assertions);
- per-turn latency in the call record;
- typed concurrency state on create;
- TypeScript and Python SDKs, an MCP server and config-as-code.
Add to the Kano must-be list; test each against the buyer interviews.

Proposed: build the contact-and-compliance layer Vapi lacks as first-class objects in VP v1:
- a contact record with consent state, suppression and history;
- a predial veto on by default, driven by our own scrub list;
- caller-ID class and DLT fields;
- per-contact calling windows in local time, retries and attempt budgets;
- business-language outcomes mapped from engineering codes, raw code kept beside.

nodes.rationale:
On Bolna, mechanisms read from bundle flags were fully right 1 time in 4:
- ambient_noise_track: no mixing code in OSS;
- use_fillers: a prompt note, and the audio path is dead code;
- synthesizer.caching: lasts one call only.
Structure inferences were 5/5 correct and docs-led ones 13/14. A flag shows that a feature is exposed, not how it works.

nodes.title:
Before inferring a closed vendor's architecture, sweep its full docs dump (llms-full / sitemap) against a fixed checklist:
- speech-to-speech mode;
- eager or speculative turn-taking;
- barge-in trigger;
- hangup modes;
- tools (incl. web search);
- multi-agent handoff;
- RAG placement;
- language ID and switching;
- telemetry;
- defaults.

Bolna language switching in code is a dedicated judge LLM:
- default claude-haiku-4-5 via LiteLLM, with a Bedrock ap-south-1 option;
- fed by a parallel streaming LID recognizer (Sarvam or Soniox);
- hedged duplicate request at 1.8 s, 6 s cap;
- speculative follow-up in the target language;
- per-language handoff clips cached across calls.
This corrects the l4a description 'code-switching is a prompt'.

Hosted-only Bolna mechanisms that the open code cannot decide:
- whether and where ambient noise is mixed;
- whether hosted fillers play audio;
- the hosted ws route and run id;
- the hosted Deepgram MIP opt-out value;
- the model behind the hosted language judge;
- the RabbitMQ scheduler's dedupe, opt-out and calling-window behaviour.

nodes.closes_when:
Account-phase capture closes it:
- a stereo recording of a default-agent call with ambient_noise and use_fillers on;
- the execution log's other_latencies model fields for language_switch / hangup_check / voicemail_check;
- the messy-CSV batch result (predictions P04a-P05).

nodes.title:
Proposed: treat ElevenLabs Agents as the strongest global rival for Indian enterprise voice. It is not just a TTS vendor: it has India residency, native Exotel, Indian GTM hires and (vendor-claimed) Indian agent logos, and a campaign product in the pipeline. Score VP against it on five runnable tasks:
- handset reply gap on Indian PSTN on a standard (non-Enterprise) plan;
- Hinglish language hold on the default configuration;
- recurring 09:00-21:00 IST windows with per-lead retries and suppression;
- messy-CSV hygiene (+91 normalisation, dedupe, opt-out);
- Indian carrier and regulatory failure reasons in plain language.

Proposed v1 compliance core, shared by every job (Kano must-be):
- (M1) per-call category classifier plus in-call promotion/solicitation fence;
- (M2) series/CLI routing, A2P declaration export and number health;
- (M4) tenant-wide opt-out/suppression with attempt caps;
- (M6) DPDP notice, recording disclosure, retention and India residency;
- non-removable AI disclosure.
Performance tier: a calling-window engine keyed by category and sector. Deferred with pre-sale: the consent/inquiry ledger. Never market NCPR scrubbing as a feature: on 140 the operator scrubs.

Indian vendors' compliance docs against primary text:
- 0 of 3 mention the Third Amendment, A2P pre-declaration (due ~2026-11-18) or 1601;
- 2 of 3 state TRAI hours as 9 AM-9 PM (Bolna for all calls; Exotel for promotional), against the primary 10:00 start, and neither separates service calls, which have no band;
- Exotel says 'voice calls do not require DLT', calls a telco disincentive a sender penalty, and classes surveys/feedback as UCC;
- all 3 put compliance on the buyer.

RBI/2026-27/230 (NBFC RBC Third Amendment Directions, effective 2027-01-01), read in primary:
- contact only 08:00-19:00;
- call time and count logs;
- two-way recordings kept 6 months, borrower told;
- excessive or out-of-hours calling deemed harsh;
- grievance officer on every recovery communication;
- IIBF-certified recovery agents;
- explicit TCCCPR compliance (100AB).
100A extends these to normal-course collection only 'wherever explicitly specified'.

Tool findings.
REA 6.7.0:
- analyze-web-bundle hung (no output, killed at 300 s and 420 s) on a proxied headless-Chromium target;
- capture-browser-scenario launch mode has no proxy option, so navigation failed;
- connect mode to our own CDP Chromium worked (115 script URLs).
TLS: trai.gov.in (emSign root) and egazette.gov.in (missing Let's Encrypt YR1 intermediate) verify with Mozilla roots plus the published YR1 chain, with no verification disabled.

Teardown lane retell (account-free), after the adversarial verifier: 67 graded claims (50 observed, 10 inferred, 5 vendor-claimed, 2 unknown) and 12 pre-registered account-phase predictions with falsifiability notes. The verifier confirmed 23, corrected 14, downgraded 4, added 7 and left 19 out-of-scope claims unchecked. Sources:
- docs llms.txt (356 pages) and llms-full.txt (338 pages split);
- the OpenAPI (109 ops, 319 schemas, revision 2026-10-19);
- pricing (re-fetched 19:27Z, unchanged), changelog, status API (50 incidents) and the Ashby job board;
- DNS/RDAP, AWS IP ranges and a client-subnet DNS re-run;
- the web SDK source via npm pack (not installed);
- a slimmed HAR of the dashboard login redirect;
- REA web-bundle analysis of the pricing page;
- 19 India-relevant community threads read in full.
Neither the lane nor the verifier could write report.md or verify.md (the harness refuses .md report files from subagents); the report and verification text are in the workflow's structured output, and the per-claim verification record is in claims.json (verification, claim_lane, grade_lane fields) and predictions.json (verifier_check).

Retell account-phase questions (after the verifier):
- dashboard surface and the Campaigns screen (retries, pacing, DNC settings);
- KYC path for team@ (a Persona ID of a real person; one ID verifies one account, so this is the owner's call);
- whether Indian card or UPI payment works;
- time from dashboard login to first call on an Indian handset via a Retell US number vs a BYO Plivo or Vobiz trunk (sign-up itself was already done by Instinct, ~10 min);
- handset reply gap on Indian PSTN (predicted p50 1.6-2.4 s) with the SDP media address from each call's PCAP (LiveKit Indian /24 or US block);
- messy-CSV behaviour on batch calls (opt-out row predicted to be dialled);
- the scope of the 4,000-token billing rule;
- voicemail detection on Indian operator messages;
- whether an API-created agent with no handbook_config still says it is an AI when asked;
- whether a 1600-series line can be trunked to Retell's US SIP edge at all.

Vapi campaigns:
- one absolute window (earliestAt/latestAt; the Dashboard allows at most 7 days ahead in 15-minute steps; one time zone per campaign);
- no recurring daily window and no per-contact retry (the only retry is waiting up to 1 h for concurrency); no draft; read-only after launch;
- the buyer owns retries, attempt budgets and suppression.
The dashboard's client-side CSV code prepends "+" to any number (a 10-digit Indian mobile becomes "+98..."), rejects the whole file on one empty number, and has no dedupe or opt-out column. The server applies an E.164 check by default, so whether a mis-prefixed number is rejected or dialled is unknown (account phase, P04).

Vapi velocity:
- 25 weekly changelog entries with 82 items from 2026-04-13 to 2026-10-12 (about 3.3 items a week);
- October 2025 to March 2026 published only as one retroactive summary;
- SDK release gaps of 4-7 months over the same winter, then v3.0.0 of both server SDKs and the web SDK in October 2026;
- nothing India-specific shipped.

Proposed: use Vapi as the developer-surface reference for the v1 parity floor, not as an Indian-market rival. Score VP against Vapi on five runnable tasks:
- domestic Indian CLI with declared A2P;
- messy-CSV hygiene (+91 normalisation, dedupe, opt-out);
- recurring 10:00-21:00 windows with per-contact retries;
- handset reply gap on Indian PSTN;
- business-language failure reasons with a Hinglish language lock.

Screenshot-reading calibration, Haiku 5.5 medium vs Sonnet 5.5 high, same prompt, same 8 screenshots (2 traps):
- recall: Haiku 76.5/78 (98%), Sonnet 78/78;
- material errors: Haiku 2 clear + 2 borderline, Sonnet 0. Haiku claimed both banners were absent on a screen that shows them, and copied Model-card numbers from a previous screenshot into one where they are cut off;
- tokens and time: Haiku 107k in 148 s, Sonnet 88k in 111 s.
Both caught the two traps: a capture named 'billing' that shows the agent builder, and an analytics page that was still loading.

Main-loop pixel check of 3 decision-critical walkthrough-synthesis claims. All 3 hold:
- Retell batch window default 00:00-23:59 Mon-Sun, with Send Now as the default, no agent selector, and Send disabled with no reason given;
- Bolna 3.5 + 0.5 + 2 = 6 cents, 30 s pulse on each line, concurrency 2;
- ElevenLabs 0 of 9 system tools active.
The ElevenLabs claim carries a caveat: that agent ('[Eval] Synthetic Clinic Demo') pre-existed from account setup by an unknown path, so it is not a proven default for dashboard-created agents.

Owner: analyse all the REA research and Instinct's walk-throughs deeply. Understand these players, then design a product better than them in every sense: UI, UX, product design, cost, speed, user flow, convenience, usability, vibe and results. It must carry everything discussed before:
- self-learning calling voice AI;
- personalisation;
- emotional, expressive, relational, human-like voice;
- meeting every client requirement with great results;
- omni-channel data and what is useful in it;
- an LLM-driven application where VP's agents handle everything and the client just talks;
- campaigns handled end to end, with a full view of each campaign and each call and AI analysis of them.
Deliver a thoughtful, deep, end-to-end product blueprint, shown visually for fast review (the owner is a visual learner). Keep learning from these companies always.

The end-to-end VP product blueprint (integrated, for owner review). Workflow wf_1fdbb02a-eb5 ran 26 agents with 0 errors:
- 12 digests: 7 vendor dossiers, the owner-requirements catalogue (68 requirements, 36 must), voice engine, market/regulation/money, learning/memory/omni-channel, VP assets;
- a spine and 9 module designs;
- 2 red teams with 57 findings (7 critical);
- an integrator that made 46 changes.
The blueprint holds 10 defining decisions, an AI workforce of 17 agents and engines with A0-A3 autonomy, India compliance by default, a latency budget of 1.5 s p50 at the handset, Base at INR 3.49-3.99/min ex-GST with a pre-registered ladder to INR 1.99, and a roadmap: Phase 0 gates, a v0.5 slice, then v1, v2 and v3. It is honest that VP loses today on speed, the first production call, raw voice, memory timing and languages.

16 owner decisions in the blueprint, each with a recommendation (research/2026-10-21/blueprint/integration.json; BLUEPRINT.md section 14). The first three set the most:
- (1) the default stack and the speed-versus-voice rule;
- (2) the Base price and unit, including signing off that v1 launches above INR 2;
- (3) the first design partners, with at least one non-BFSI.
The others: in-product number rental, the self-learning envelope, preference-memory tier, the Operator's name, failover, the runtime split, regional languages, benchmark voices, fixed-cost recovery, spends before 2026-11-14, who builds v0.5, assisted onboarding, and the opening spec.

Campaigns test suites: 41 of 41 pass (30 against Postgres 16 on the throwaway cluster, 11 pure). They cover:
- every gate code;
- T3 at the form, the HTTP API (422 with the rule id) and the gate;
- cross-midnight rules;
- 12 concurrent workers racing for 5 attempts, each claimed exactly once;
- reservations released on refusal;
- tenancy isolation;
- launch approval voided by a policy-version change.
Run twice on the same database with the kernel suites, 62 of 62 both times.

Dial gate after the 'who gets called' review (WGC-01..08), each fix fail-closed:
- consent is decided by the newest record across the purposes covering the call, ordered by evidence.statedAt when it is ISO;
- an unreadable expiry counts as expired;
- promotional calls need an explicit fresh not_registered (7 days), or registered plus CRF;
- a +91 number keeps the IST quiet hours and bands whatever zone was imported;
- an erased contact is refused at G2;
- an unknown dial with a live call holds a line.

nodes.method:
Read 7 re-shot screenshots at dd274a3 (approval-d, call-d, operator-plan-d, campaign-draft-d, settings-activity-m, agent-stack-d, contact-d) and checked each against decision.2026-10-21.no-developer-text-in-ui. Leaks found:
- activity feed: tool ids with the dots turned into spaces ('ran test simulate', 'VP ran attempt retry per ladder');
- call page: 'did not return JSON' and 'None; they are suppressed';
- cost basis: a file path and an ISO date;
- stack card: 'first token', 'bake-off', 'ungated', 'the owner's pick';
- contact page: 'Asia/Kolkata', 'Dnd unknown', the raw code 'te', ISO dates, 'Fresh: No';
- campaign header: 'policy v1'.
The browser scan missed them because it only matched dotted tool ids. Not reviewed: the other 19 screen pairs.

Public documentation pages only (no Google call with credentials), read by a research agent on 2026-10-21 and checked against the runtime design. Sources (docs.cloud.google.com; cloud.google.com 301-redirects there):
- live-api/start-manage-session: regional host, LlmBidiService path (v1), bearer token; session limits (audio 15 min without compression, connection ~10 min, goAway 60 s before end, resumption within 24 h); contextWindowCompression trigger_tokens 5k-128k.
- live-api/configure-language-voice: setup fields in snake_case in the samples (generation_config, speech_config.voice_config.prebuilt_voice_config.voice_name, input_audio_transcription, output_audio_transcription, realtime_input_config.automatic_activity_detection); 30 voices incl. Kore, Charon, Puck, Callirrhoe, Aoede, Zephyr, Leda.
- live-api/get-started-websocket: setup.model is the full model resource path; the sample's realtime input uses media_chunks with audio/pcm;rate=16000.
- live-api (overview) and models/gemini/3-8-live: audio in raw PCM16 16 kHz LE, out PCM16 24 kHz LE; gemini-3.8-live GA, released 2026-09-24, regions us, eu, us-central1; gemini-live-2.5-flash-native-audio GA, US and EU regions only, retires 2026-12-13; gemini-3.5-transcribe-live-preview is preview.
- learn/locations: lists Live models in every group, including Asia Pacific; this conflicts with the per-model pages, which are more specific.
- text-to-speech/docs/gemini-tts: Cloud TTS v1/text:synthesize with model_name, or Vertex generateContent with speech_config; regions global/us/eu/northamerica-northeast1; Gemini 3.8 Flash TTS preview only via the Gemini Enterprise API.
Not confirmed:
- whether Vertex accepts camelCase JSON on the websocket (proto JSON normally does; the runtime sends camelCase);
- the exact server message keys (setupComplete, serverContent.inputTranscription/outputTranscription, interrupted, usageMetadata);
- realtimeInput.audio versus mediaChunks (runtime default mediaChunks, switch with VERTEX_LIVE_AUDIO);
- whether interimInputTranscription exists on Vertex;
- a global host for Live.

